perovskite structure already showed first indications of degradation via the peak at $\scriptstyle q=9{\mathsf{n m}}^{-1}$ by the 10 min time point. We suspect that the absence of the crystalline precursor structure may be due to a more rapid transition in air as compared to nitrogen. We observe similar behavior, i.e., the absence of the crystalline precursor for $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3-x}\mathsf{C l}_{x}$ on a $M B C P\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ superstructure annealed in air, albeit with more severe degradation into $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ (Figure S3, Supporting Information).
At the beginning of the experiment, the crystalline precursor was detected in the GIWAXS profile via the peak at $q\approx11~\mathsf{n m}^{-1}$ ; see also GIWAXS pattern in Figure 4a showing results for the 3 min $(30~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ time point. At the 50 min time point $(80^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ a scattering ring
## ARTICLE
appeared at $q=10\ \mathsf{n m}^{-1}$ (Figure 4b), indicating the transition from the crystalline precursor to polycrystalline perovskite. This structural transition was completed at the 60 min time point $(90\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ Figure 4c), similar to the 10 min time point for the ITD annealed $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in nitrogen (compare with Figure 3c). Perovskite film degradation was observed at the 70 min time point after the temperature was raised to the final value of $100^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ and progressed until the final time point at $120\mathrm{min}$ (Figure 4d). Similar to the ITD annealing in nitrogen, three distinct structures were identified during the TTD processing of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3-x}\mathsf{C l}_{x}$ in $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ superstructures: (1) crystalline precursor, (2) perovskite, and (3) $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ as a perovskite degradation product.
From the present and previous work, we conclude that transitions between these different structures are driven by a combination of annealing temperature, environmental atmosphere and film thickness.22,26,27 A yet-to-be identified crystalline precursor structure was observed upon spin coating the $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3-x}\mathsf{C l}_{x}$ precursor solution on $M B C P-A l_{2}O_{3},$ and displayed noteworthy thermal stability during the TTD ramp annealing. We postulate that the crystalline precursor undergoes a solid solid phase transformation to the 3D perovskite structure at $80^{\circ}C$ . This transition occurs relatively rapidly $(<10\mathsf{m i n})$ and is largely completed at $90~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . To the best of our knowledge, this is the first experimental observation of a transformation from a crystalline precursor to the perovskite crystal structure for methylammonium lead trihalide perovskite materials, suggesting a phase transformation pathway consistent with Ostwald's “Rule of Stages” in which a metastable precursor is first formed, followed by transformation into the more stable product.59,60
We further note that macroscopic coverage of the substrate with perovskite layers is largely affected by the heat treatment profile. In plan view SEM images (Figure $\mathsf{S4a,b},$ Supporting Information) we observe large $(>2\ \mu\mathsf{m})$ perovskite crystallite islands and pores in the capping layer on $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ under ITD annealing conditions. In contrast, the discrete perovskite islands and pores are on the submicrometer length scale for the TTD annealed MBCP- ${\cdot\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}}$ perovskite film shown in Figure S4d,e (Supporting Information). Since the only difference in the heat treatment profile is at the initial stage before perovskite formation, this result suggests that the crystalline precursor coverage and the subsequent phase transformation contributes significantly to the final film coverage, which in turn greatly affects device performance (vide infra).
$M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ Perovskite Solar Cell Performance. Figure 5 shows SEM images of $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite films prepared from 40 wt $\%$ precursor solutions and annealed either instantaneously at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (ITD), or with a slow temperature ramp to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (TTD) in nitrogen. While the crystallite domain sizes are very similar for the ITD and TTD annealed films (Figure 5c,d), the capping film coverage is drastically different. Figure 5a shows that large pores $(>1\ \mu\mathsf{m})$ were formed in the capping layer of the $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite films annealed instantly at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (ITD). In contrast, when the $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite film was annealed with a slow temperature ramp of $5~^{\circ}C/5$ min in nitrogen to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and held for $45\mathrm{\min}$ at the final temperature (TTD), significantly better perovskite film coverage was achieved. The 40 wt $\%$ perovskite film morphology is highly similar to the $20\mathsf{w t}\%$ perovskite films (Figure S4, Supporting Information). We hypothesize that the crystalline precursor, which is present for a longer dwell period during TTD annealing, promotes film formation with fewer macroscopic defects than in ITD annealed films.
We fabricated $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite solar cells annealed under ITD and TTD conditions (see Table S1, Supporting Information). Comparing the best performing MBCP- ${\bf\cdot A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ perovskite solar cells in Figure 5e, the values of short-circuit current density $(J_{5C})$ , open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ , and fill factor (FF) of the $\phantom{0}\overline{{\Pi\mathsf{D}\ –N_{2}}}$ device increased by at least $15\%$ relative to the ITD-air device.
At the same time, the power conversion efficiency increased from 5.2 to $8.3\%$ . We attribute the device performance improvement to the enhanced perovskite film morphology and coverage, enabling more uniform charge generation and collection, and reduced leakage with fewer available shunt paths.19,22,26,27
## CONCLUSION
In conclusion, we employed in situ 2D GIWAXS in combination with SEM to follow the structural evolution of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b l}_{3-x}\mathsf{C l}_{x}$ perovskite on mesoporous block copolymer $A|_{2}O_{3}$ thin films under thermal annealing. Solution-processed perovskite films underwent transitions between three distinct crystalline structures during thermal annealing: a crystalline precursor, a perovskite, and a degradation product in the form of ${\mathsf{P b l}}_{2}$ . Finally, we demonstrated by annealing the $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite films with a well-controlled temperature ramp in a dry environment that the perovskite capping film coverage and the power conversion efficiency of block copolymer-directed alumina perovskite solar cells can be greatly improved. To the best of our knowledge, the crystalline precursor has not previously been described, and its exact structure is currently unknown. We showed that the evolution between these structures markedly depends on the annealing conditions. A clear understanding of $\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{N H}_{3}\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{X}_{3}$ wet solution processing conditions in combination with bicontinuous BCP gyroidal titania electrodes37,38 with an interconnecting pore network may allow complete perovskite infiltration, and enhance the photogenerated electron collection and transport for optimal solar cell performance.24,3234
## METHODS
Materials. All materials were used as received. Anhydrous grades of toluene, $\boldsymbol{n}$ -butanol, tetrahydrofuran, chloroform, $N,N$ -dimethylformamide and terpineol, $97\%$ aluminum tri-secbutoxide, ${>}97\%$ titanium isopropoxide, 20 wt $\%$ aluminum oxide nanoparticles in isopropanol $(<50\ \mathsf{n m}$ , product number 702129), 57 wt $\%$ hydriodic acid in water, 33 wt $\%$ methylamine solution in ethanol, and lead chloride were obtained from Sigma-Aldrich. $70\%$ nitric acid and $37\%$ hydrochloric acid were obtained from Mallinckrodt Baker and EMD Millipore, respectively. 10 and $46~{\mathsf{c P}}$ ethyl cellulose were obtained from TCI America.
Mesoporous Block Copolymer-Directed Alumina $(M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3})$ and Titania $(M B C P-T i O_{2})$ ) Films. $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ thin films were prepared using $50~\mathsf{m g}$ of poly(isoprene)-block-poly(styrene)-block-poly(ethylene oxide) (ISO, $M_{n}=38.3~\mathrm{kg/mol}$ , with a polydispersity index of 1.07 containing 68.4 wt $\%$ PI and 18.0 wt $\%$ PS) dissolved in $500~\mathrm{mg}$ of toluene and $\boldsymbol{n}$ -butanol solvent mixture (1:1, w/w). In a separate vial, $104~\mathsf{m g}$ of $97\%$ aluminum tri-secbutoxide, $478{\ m g}$ of $\boldsymbol{n}$ -butanol, and $478{\mathrm{~mg}}$ of toluene were added sequentially. The white cloudy suspension was left undisturbed for $15\mathrm{\min}.$ , followed by vigorous stirring for $30~\mathrm{min}$ to allow homogenization. $55\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $70\%$ nitric acid was added, and the mixture was stirred overnight $(>12\ h)$ . The transparent alumina sol was added into the ISO solution and stirred for $45\mathrm{{min}}$ . The ${\mathsf{I S O}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ solution was processed by spin coating on silicon at 2000 rpm (45 s) in a nitrogen drybox. The $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ hybrid thin films were baked at $50^{\circ}C$ (2 h), $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (2 h), and $130^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\left(2\mathsf{h}\right)$ sequentially in the drybox and calcined in a tube furnace at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (3 h) with a ramp rate of $1\ {^{\circ}C}/{\mathsf{m i n}}$ .
MBCP- $\cdot\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ thin films were prepared using a modified method as described elsewhere.38 Briefly, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ of ISO was dissolved in $1.35~{\mathfrak{g}}$ of tetrahydrofuran and chloroform solvent mixture (4:5, w/w). In a separate vial, $53.9\mu\mathrm{L}$ of ${>}97\%$ titanium isopropoxide, $16.8\mu\mathrm{L}$ of $37\%$ HCl acid, and $216\mu\mathrm{L}$ of tetrahydrofuran were mixed and stirred vigorously. The yellow-colored titania sol was added into the ISO solution and stirred for $45\mathsf{m i n}$ . The $150-\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ solution was processed by spin coating on silicon at 2000 rpm $(45~5)$ in the drybox. The $M B C P\mathrm{-}\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ hybrid thin films were baked at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (2 h), $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (2 h), and calcined in a tube furnace at $450\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\ (3\mathsf{h})$ with a ramp rate of $1\ {}^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{m i n}}$ .
Mesoporous Alumina Nanoparticle (NP) Films. Mesoporous ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ nanoparticle (NP) thin films were prepared as described elsewhere.1820 The binder-free mesoporous ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ NP film was prepared by spin coating 6.67 wt $\%\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ NPs in isopropanol on silicon at 2500 rpm (60 s), and baked at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (1 h). The 2 wt % ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ NP-binder paste was prepared by mixing $\textsf{19}$ of $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}\mathsf{N P s},3.33\mathsf{g}$ of terpineol, $\boldsymbol{1}\L_{9}$ of $10{\mathsf{c P}}$ ethyl cellulose and 1 g of $46\mathsf{c P}$ ethyl cellulose in $43.67~9$ of isopropanol. The mixture was stirred vigorously at $70~^{\circ}C$ for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . The mesoporous $A|_{2}O_{3}$ NP film with binder was prepared by spin coating the ${\mathsf{A l}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ NP-binder mixture on silicon at 2500 rpm $_{(60~5)}$ and calcined in a tube furnace at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ (3 h) with a ramp rate of $1\ ^{\circ}C/\mathsf{m i n}$ .
Mesoporous Alumina Thin Film Characterization. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images were acquired on Au Pd coated mesoporous alumina thin films using LEO 1550 and TESCAN MIRA3-LM field emission SEMs equipped with in-lens detectors. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) images were obtained on a Veeco Multimode II SPM with a Nanoscope III controller in tapping mode at ambient conditions. GISAXS was measured at the G1 beamline of the Cornell High Energy Synchrotron Source (CHESS). The G1 beamline setup consists of a multilayer monochromator of wavelength $\lambda=0.1225\ \mathrm{nm}$ with a CCD area detector with a $71.73\:\mu\mathrm{m}$ pixel size and a total of $1024\times$ 1024 pixels. The sample-to-detector distance was $2.745\mathrm{~m~}$ . The incident angle of the beam was varied between $0.1^{\circ}$ and $0.3^{\circ}$ with typical exposure times ${<}2\thinspace s$ . GISAXS patterns were analyzed with an in-house software61 and the FIT2D program.62
Wide-Angle X-ray Scattering Measurements of Methylammonium Lead Tri-iodide/Chloride Hybrid Perovskite. Methylammonium iodide $(C H_{3}N H_{3}l)$ was prepared using 57 wt $\%$ hydriodic acid (HI) in water and 33 wt $\%$ methylamine solution $(C H_{3}N H_{2})$ in ethanol as reported elsewhere.18 5 40 wt $\%$ of methylammonium iodide $(C H_{3}N H_{3}l)$ and lead chloride $(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{C l}_{2})$ (3:1 by molarity) dissolved in $N,N$ -dimethylformamide was dispensed on the MBCP- ${\bf\cdot A l}_{2}{\bf O}_{3}$ and flat glass coverslip substrates by spin coating at 3000 rpm (45 s) in air. For ex situ measurements, the hybrid perovskite samples were annealed by a slow temperature ramp of $10~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}/10$ min and held at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $45\mathrm{\min}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere. The annealed perovskite films were carefully scratched from the substrate and powdered for X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis. The XRD characterization was performed on a Rigaku Ultima IV multipurpose X-ray diffraction system using $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K a}$ radiation $(40\mathsf{k V},44\mathsf{m A},$ , wavelength $\lambda{=}1.5418\mathrm{\AA}$ ) and unit cell refinement analysis with the MDI Jade 9 software.
For in situ GIWAXS measurements, after spin coating the sample was immediately loaded on a temperature-controlled stage at the D1 beamline of CHESS. The D1 beamline setup consists of a multilayer monochromator of wavelength $\lambda=$ 0.1161 nm. GIWAXS patterns were collected on Fuji image plates placed in a holder at a distance of $177~\mathrm{mm}$ from the sample. The incident beam angle was above the silicon critical angle $(\sim0.25^{\circ})$ with exposure times ${<}5~\mathsf{s}$ . The direct beam was blocked with lead tape. The exposed plates were scanned for digital processing with a GE Healthcare Typhoon FLA-7000
## ARTICLE
image plate reader. Digital images were analyzed using the FIT2D program.62 The resolution of the letter-sized image plate is $2000\times2500$ pixels with a $100\mu\mathrm{{m}}$ pixel size. The measurements in nitrogen were conducted in a custom-made environmental chamber mounted on the temperature-controlled stage.58
Isothermal-Time-Dependent (ITD) Perovskite Crystallization. $\mathsf{A20w t\%}$ precursor solution was dispensed on the $M B C P-A l_{2}O_{3}$ substrate by spin coating and immediately loaded on the temperaturestage held at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at the D1 beamline. GIWAXS measurements were taken at different time intervals over a dwell of $50~\mathrm{min}$ . The measurements were conducted in ambient air and nitrogen.
Time Temperature-Dependent (TTD) Perovskite Crystallization. A 20 wt $\%$ precursor solution was dispensed on the MBCP- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ substrate by spin coating and loaded on the temperature-stage held at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ at the D1 beamline. The stage was slowly heated at a ramp rate of $10^{\circ}\mathsf{C}/10$ min to the final temperature of $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite sample was held at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 60 min. The GIWAXS measurements were taken at different time intervals in nitrogen.
$M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ Hybrid Perovskite Solar Cell Assembly. $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite solar cells $(>20$ cells for each annealing history) were fabricated as reported elsewhere.18 Briefly, HCl-etched FTO glass substrates were coated with a dense $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ compact layer prepared by spin coating a mildly HCl-acidified solution of titanium isopropoxide in ethanol, and sintered at $500~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $45\mathrm{\min}$ . The $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ hybrid films were prepared by spin coating on the $T_{1}0_{2}/\mathsf{F T O}$ substrates at 2000 rpm (45 s). The substrates were baked at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (2 h), $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (2 h), and $130^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (2 h) sequentially in the drybox and calcined at $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (3 h) with a ramp rate of $1\ ^{\circ}C/\mathsf{m i n}$ .
A 40 wt $\%$ perovskite precursor solution was dispensed on the $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ scaffold by spin coating at 2000 rpm (45 s). For the ITD-air processed MBCP- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ perovskite solar cells, the devices were fabricated in ambient air and heated in an oven at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $45\mathrm{\min}$ . For the TTD-nitrogen processed $M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ perovskite solar cells, the devices were fabricated in a nitrogen glovebox. After drying at ambient temperature for at least $20~\mathrm{min}$ , the samples were slowly heated on a hot plate from ambient temperature to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ at a ramp rate of $5^{\circ}C/$ 5 min, and held at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 45 min. The electron blocking layer was formed by spin coating $80\mathrm{\mM}2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(N,N-di $p$ - methoxyphenylamine)- $\cdot9,9^{\prime}$ -spirobifluorene (spiro-OMeTAD) in chlorobenzene solution with $68.3~\mathsf{m M}$ tert-butylpyridine and $22.6~\mathsf{m M}$ lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide additives $(170\mathrm{mg/mL}$ in acetonitrile) and aged overnight in a desiccator (in air). Silver contact electrodes of $150\ \mathsf{n m}$ were thermally evaporated to complete the devices.
$M B C P\cdot A l_{2}O_{3}$ Hybrid Perovskite Solar Cell Characterization. More than 20 solar cells were measured for each annealing condition (ITD versus TTD) with a Keithley 2400 under AM 1.5G $100\mathrm{\mW/cm}^{2}$ simulated sunlight (Abet Technologies Sun 2000) calibrated against an NREL certified KG5 filtered silicon reference diode. The cells were masked with a square aperture defining an active area of typically $0.07\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ and measured in a light-tight sample holder.
